@page "/reset"

@*
    The reset-view escape: the addressable, always-reachable way to forget
    everything the Explorer remembers about how you like it and where you were.

    It lives at its own route rather than only as a control in the chrome,
    because the user who needs it most is usually one whose remembered state is
    the problem, so the escape must not depend on that chrome rendering. A typed
    or bookmarked address always works.

    Resetting is an explicit action, never something that happens on arrival,
    so following a link cannot silently discard somebody's preferences. It
    clears only this identity's own preferences for the connected cluster.
*@

<PageTitle>Reset view</PageTitle>

<h1>Reset view</h1>

@if (_reset)
{
    <p role="status">
        The Explorer has forgotten your remembered view. It will start from its
        default the next time you open it.
    </p>
    <p>
        <a class="lt-btn" href="./">Back to the Explorer</a>
    </p>
}
else
{
    <p>
        The Explorer remembers a few things about how you like it and where you
        were last time. Resetting forgets all of them for the account and cluster
        you are connected to now, and affects nothing else - no cluster data, and
        no sign-in.
    </p>

    <h2>What is remembered</h2>
    <ul>
        @foreach (var key in Preferences.Keys)
        {
            <li>@key.Description</li>
        }
    </ul>

    <div class="lt-dialog__actions">
        <a class="lt-btn lt-btn--quiet" href="./">Cancel</a>
        <LtButton Disabled="_busy" OnClick="_resetClicked">Reset view</LtButton>
    </div>
}
